\pard\sa120\sb120\fs24\b Get OneView connection status\b0{\*\bkmkstart get-oneview-connection-status}{\*\bkmkend get-oneview-connection-status}\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 Quick reachability + authentication check against a OneView appliance, with optional per-server status. Read-only - safe during a change freeze. \b This command never prompts.\b0 When run without parameters, the command checks for an existing OneView session (established via {\f1 Connect-OneView\f0}) and uses that appliance automatically - no connect/disconnect. Reachability probes {\f1 GET /rest/version\f0} (no auth); authentication probes {\f1 GET /rest/server-hardware\f0} with the session token or supplied credentials. Use {\f1 -ServerIdentifier\f0} to also report a single server's power/health. If no session exists, the command returns an error telling you to connect first with {\f1 Connect-OneView -OneViewHost <oneview-appliance-host>\f0}.\par
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # BARE: no params - reports the ACTIVE OneView session (from Connect-OneView).# Never prompts. If nothing is connected it reports not connected.Get-OneViewConnectionStatus\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # WITH -OneViewHost: check a SPECIFIC appliance instead of the active session.# Connectivity + appliance version + managed server countGet-OneViewConnectionStatus -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -IncludeServerCount\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Specific server status by name (still targets the named appliance)Get-OneViewConnectionStatus -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -ServerIdentifier srv01\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Specific server status by serial number (resolved via OneView)Get-OneViewConnectionStatus -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -ServerIdentifier MXQ1234567 -IdentifierType Serial# SAME RESULT with a single parameter - -IdentifierType defaults to Auto and# detects the serial automatically, so you never need to specify the type:Get-OneViewConnectionStatus -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -ServerIdentifier MXQ1234567\f0}\par}
\pard\li360\ri360\sa80\fs22\i \b Supplying {\f1 -OneViewHost\f0} vs not:\b0 with no parameters the command reports the\par\nactive {\f1 Connect-OneView\f0} session - the appliance it connects to is whatever you\par\nalready connected to, and it never asks for a host or credentials. Supply\par\n{\f1 -OneViewHost\f0} to check a \i different\i0 appliance (it will use {\f1 -Credential\f0} /\par\n{\f1 ONEVIEW_USER\f0}+{\f1 ONEVIEW_PASSWORD\f0} for that host; it still never prompts). Omitting\par\nthe host is the normal "is my connection still up?" check.\i0\par
