\pard\sa120\sb120\fs24\b Get OneView server list\b0{\*\bkmkstart get-oneview-server-list}{\*\bkmkend get-oneview-server-list}\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 Lists every server managed by the appliance with normalised connection/health fields. Pagination is handled internally so the full fleet is returned in one call. Supports an optional {\f1 -Filter\f0} to narrow by health, power state, maintenance mode, or name.\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 Each row also shows the server's \b HPE OneView maintenance mode\b0 and lifecycle \b state\b0, which is what a hardware engineer needs to tell a server that is intentionally in maintenance (e.g. for firmware work) apart from one that is merely monitored or has hit an error:\par
\trowd\trgaph60\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx3008\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx6016\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx9024{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 \clcbpat3 \b \b0 Column\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 \clcbpat3 \b \b0 Source field\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 \clcbpat3 \b \b0 Meaning\cell}\row\trowd\trgaph60\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx3008\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx6016\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx9024{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 MaintMode\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 MaintenanceModeEnabled\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 Yes\f0} when the server is IN HPE OneView maintenance mode, otherwise {\f1 No\f0}. This is the definitive "in/out of maintenance?" flag.\cell}\row\trowd\trgaph60\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx3008\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx6016\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx9024{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 State\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 state\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 Lifecycle state: {\f1 Monitored\f0} (normal), {\f1 MaintenanceMode\f0}, {\f1 ConfigureHardware\f0}, {\f1 NoProfileApplied\f0}, {\f1 ProfileApplying\f0}, {\f1 ProfileApplied\f0}, {\f1 ProfileError\f0}, {\f1 Deleting\f0}.\cell}\row\trowd\trgaph60\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx3008\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx6016\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx9024{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 State Reason\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 stateReason\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 Optional free-text reason for the current state (often populated for maintenance).\cell}\row\trowd\trgaph60\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx3008\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx6016\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs\cellx9024{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 Model\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 {\f1 modelNumber\f0}\cell}{\pard\fi0\li60\ri60\fs22\sa40\sb40 Short, stable HPE model \b code\b0 (e.g. {\f1 867963-B21\f0}) - placed last because it varies in length and would otherwise break column alignment. The full descriptive model string is still available in the {\f1 -PassThru\f0} object as {\f1 model\f0}.\cell}\row
\pard\li360\ri360\sa80\fs22\i \b Maintenance mode vs other states:\b0 {\f1 MaintMode = Yes\f0} \b only\b0 when the server is in maintenance mode (and {\f1 State\f0} will read {\f1 MaintenanceMode\f0}). A server in {\f1 ProfileError\f0}, {\f1 Monitored\f0}, {\f1 NoProfileApplied\f0}, etc. shows in {\f1 State\f0} but {\f1 MaintMode\f0} stays {\f1 No\f0} - so the two columns are independent and an engineer reads {\f1 MaintMode = Yes\f0} as the definitive maintenance signal. None of your fleet being in maintenance is expected when every row reads {\f1 MaintMode = No\f0}; you would only see {\f1 MaintenanceMode\f0} in {\f1 State\f0} (and {\f1 MaintMode = Yes\f0}) for a server that's actually been placed into maintenance.\par\n\par\n\b Note on dates:\b0 OneView maintenance mode is a manual toggle on the server-hardware resource - there is \b no start/end timestamp\b0 for it. Scheduled maintenance \i windows\i0 with start/end dates come from SCOM (see {\f1 Get-MaintenanceStatusReport\f0}), not from OneView.\i0\par
\pard\sa80\fs22 \b Connection behaviour (shared helper):\b0 An existing OneView connection always takes priority - if a session is already active, the command reuses it and never reconnects (reconnecting could drop the live session and cause incidents); if you supplied a different {\f1 -OneViewHost\f0}, it warns you which appliance you are connected to and to run {\f1 Disconnect-OneView\f0} first to switch. When nothing is connected, supplying {\f1 -OneViewHost\f0} establishes a persistent session automatically, prompting for username and password interactively as needed (exactly like {\f1 Test-ServerConnectivity\f0}). If there is no host and no active session, it returns an exception explaining there is none and how to connect. The session persists - this command never disconnects (only {\f1 Disconnect-OneView\f0} does).\par
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Full list of servers from current HPEOneView session (no params needed if connected)Get-OneViewServerList\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Full list of servers connected to the applianceGet-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Narrow to critical-health or powered-on serversGet-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'health:Critical'Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'power:On'\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Narrow to servers currently IN or OUT of OneView maintenance modeGet-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'maintenance:Yes'Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'maintenance:No'# Condensed summary view (Server Name, Serial, MaintMode, Health, iLO IP only)Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Summary# Explicit full-field view (default when no switch is supplied)Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Detail\f0}\par}
{\pard\sa80\sb80\fs20\fi0\brdrt\brdrs\brdrw10\brdrb\brdrs\brdrw10\clcbpat2\cf2{\f1 # Narrow by partial / wildcard server name (substring AND * / ? wildcards)Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'name:PROD'Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'name:PROD-*'Get-OneViewServerList -OneViewHost oneview.example.com -Filter 'name:srv-0?'\f0}\par}
\pard\li360\ri360\sa80\fs22\i \b Reference:\b0 the fields above come straight from the HPE OneView REST API {\f1 GET /rest/server-hardware\f0} resource. See the official HPE OneView REST API reference for {\f1 ServerHardware\f0} ({\f1 MaintenanceModeEnabled\f0}, {\f1 state\f0}, {\f1 stateReason\f0}) and the maintenance-mode operations:\par\n- HPE OneView REST API (ServerHardware resource): <https://developer.hpe.com/blog/hpe-oneview-rest-api>\par\n- HPE OneView documentation / API explorer: <https://oneview.ext.hpe.com/>\par\n- Enable/Disable server maintenance mode ({\f1 Enable-OVMaintenanceMode\f0} / {\f1 Disable-OVMaintenanceMode\f0} in the HPEOneView PowerShell library): <https://github.com/HewlettPackard/Powershell-HPE-OneView>\i0\par
